- How would you tune thresholds to minimize false positives for bursts?
- How would you validate end-to-end remediation without impacting real users?

## Why This Is Asked
Threats include prompt tampering and replay, which undermine model safety and tenant isolation. A signed prompt ensures integrity and provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant HMAC keys in a KMS
- Nonce/sequence replay protection
- Key rotation and revocation workflow
- Audit logging and tamper-detection tests
- Latency budget impact and mitigation

## Code Example
```javascript
function signPrompt(tenantId, payload, key) {
  const nonce = crypto.randomBytes(12).toString('hex');
  const mac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key).update(tenantId + '|' + nonce + '|' + payload).digest('hex');
  return { tenantId, nonce, payload, mac };
}

function verifyPrompt(signed, key) {
  const { tenantId, nonce, payload, mac } = signed;
  // fetch current key for tenantId from KMS in real implementation
  const expectedMac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key).update(tenantId + '|' + nonce + '|' + payload).digest('hex');
  return mac === expectedMac;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model key rotation without disrupting in-flight prompts?
- How would you handle cross-tenant alerts if verification fails frequently?

